WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.300
Last week, I put up this video about solving
the game Wordle, or at least trying to solve

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:09.410
it, using information theory, and I wanted
to add a quick...what should we call this?

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:10.410
An addendum?

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.410
A confession?

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:14.429
Basically, I just want to explain a place
where I made a mistake.

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:18.840
It turns out there was a very slight bug in
the code that I was running to recreate Wordle

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.710
and then run all of the algorithms to solve
it and test their performance.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.499
It's one of those bugs that affect a very
small percentage of cases, so it was easy

00:00:26.499 --> 00:00:30.960
to miss, and it has only a very slight effect
that, for the most part, doesn't really matter.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.370
Basically, it had to do with how you assign
a color to a guess that has multiple different

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.370
letters in it.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:40.610
For example, if you guess "speed" and the
true answer is "abide", how should you color

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:42.460
those two E's from the guess?

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.670
Well, the way that it works with the Wordle
conventions is that the first e would be colored

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.230
yellow, and the second one would be colored
gray.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.000
You might think of that first one as matching
up with something from the true answer, and

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.320
the grayness is telling you there is no second
e.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.560
By contrast, if the answer was something like
"erase", both of those e's would be colored

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.120
yellow, telling you that there is a first
e in a different location, and there's a second

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.070
e also in a different location.

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:11.970
Similarly, if one of the e's hits and it's
green, then that second one would be gray

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:17.420
in the case where the true answer has no second
e, but it would be yellow in the case where

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.680
there is a second e, and it's just in a different
location.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.940
Long story short, somewhere along the way,
I accidentally introduced behavior that differs

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.600
from these conventions slightly.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.800
And honestly, it was really dumb.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.540
Basically, at some point in the middle of
the project, I wanted to speed up some of

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.620
the computations, and I was trying a little
trick for how it computed the value for this

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:41.350
pattern between any given pair of words, and
I just didn't really think it through, and

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.260
it introduced this slight change.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.490
The ironic part is that in the end, the actual
way to make things fastest is to precompute

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.380
all those patterns so that everything is just
a lookup.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:53.670
So it wouldn't matter how long it takes to
do each one, especially if you're writing

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.940
hard-to-read buggy code to make it happen.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.049
You live and you learn.

00:01:58.049 --> 00:02:02.240
As far as how this affects the actual video,
I mean, very little of substance really changes.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.010
Of course, the main lessons about what is
information, what is entropy, all that stays

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.010
the same.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:11.239
Every now and then, if I'm showing on screen
some distribution associated with a given

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:15.499
word, that distribution might actually be
a little bit off, because some of the buckets

00:02:15.499 --> 00:02:20.139
associated with various patterns should include
either more or fewer true answers.

00:02:20.139 --> 00:02:23.870
Even then, it doesn't really come up, because
it was very rare that I would be showing a

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:27.780
word that had multiple letters that also hit
this edge case.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.390
But one of the very few things of substance
that does change, and that arguably does matter

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:37.900
a fair bit, was the final conclusion around
how if we want to find the optimal possible

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:43.220
score for the Wordle answer list, what opening
guess does such an algorithm use?

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.279
In the video, I said the best performance
that I could find came from opening with the

00:02:47.279 --> 00:02:51.879
word "crane", which was true only in the sense
that the algorithms were playing a very slightly

00:02:51.879 --> 00:02:53.310
different game.

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:57.499
After fixing it and rerunning it all, there
is a different answer for what the theoretically

00:02:57.499 --> 00:03:01.139
optimal first guess is for this particular
list.

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:06.540
And look, I know that you know that the point
of the video is not to find some technically

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.709
optimal answer to some random online game.

00:03:09.709 --> 00:03:13.760
The point of the video is to shamelessly hop
on the bandwagon of an internet trend to sneak

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.849
attack people with an information theory lesson.

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:18.040
And that's all good, I stand by that part.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.659
But I know how the Internet works, and for
a lot of people the one main takeaway was

00:03:22.659 --> 00:03:25.340
what is the best opener for the game Wordle.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:26.340
And I get it.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.430
I walked into that because I put it in the
thumbnail.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:32.129
But presumably you can forgive me if I want
to add a little correction here.

00:03:32.129 --> 00:03:35.980
And a more meaningful reason to circle back
to all this, actually, is that I never really

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.439
talked about what went into that final analysis,
and it's interesting as a sub-lesson in its

00:03:40.439 --> 00:03:42.699
own right, so that's worth doing here.

00:03:42.699 --> 00:03:47.269
Now, if you'll recall, most of our time last
video was spent on the challenge of trying

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:52.249
to write an algorithm to solve Wordle that
did not use the official list of all possible

00:03:52.249 --> 00:03:53.409
answers.

00:03:53.409 --> 00:03:56.279
To my taste, that feels a bit like overfitting
to a test set.

00:03:56.279 --> 00:03:58.989
And what's more fun is building something
that's resilient.

00:03:58.989 --> 00:04:02.779
This is why we went through the whole process
of looking at relative word frequencies in

00:04:02.779 --> 00:04:07.280
the English language to come up with some
notion of how likely each one would be to

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.349
be included as a final answer.

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:13.159
However, for what we're doing here, where
we're just trying to find an absolute best

00:04:13.159 --> 00:04:18.030
performance, period, I am incorporating that
official list and just shamelessly overfitting

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.400
to the test set.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:23.520
Which is to say we know with certainty whether
a word is included or not, and we can assign

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:26.470
a uniform probability to each one.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:30.360
If you'll remember, the first step in all
of this was to say for a particular opening

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.819
guess, maybe something like my old favorite
"crane", how likely is it that you would see

00:04:34.819 --> 00:04:36.819
each of the possible patterns?

00:04:36.819 --> 00:04:40.830
And in this context where we are shamelessly
overfitting to the Wordle answer list, all

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:45.300
that involves is counting how many of the
possible answers give each one of these patterns.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:49.330
And then, of course, most of our time was
spent on this kind of funny-looking formula

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:53.040
to quantify the amount of information that
you would get from this guest.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.030
That basically involves going through each
one of those buckets and saying, how much

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:01.780
information would you gain that has this log
expression that is a fanciful way of saying,

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:05.400
how many times would you cut your space of
possibilities in half?

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:10.080
If you observed a given pattern, we take a
weighted average of all of those, and it gives

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.110
us a measure of how much we expect to learn
from this first guess.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.270
In a moment, we'll go deeper than this.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:19.770
But if you simply search through all 13,000
different words that you could start with

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:24.430
and you ask which one has the highest expected
information, it turns out the best possible

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:31.710
answer is "soare", which doesn't really look
like a real word, but I guess it's an obsolete

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:33.370
term for a baby Hawk.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:38.230
The top 15 openers by this metric happen to
look like this.

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:42.220
But these are not necessarily the best opening
guesses, because they're only looking one

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:47.129
step in with a heuristic of expected information
to try to estimate what the true score will

00:05:47.129 --> 00:05:48.129
be.

00:05:48.129 --> 00:05:51.640
But there are few enough patterns that we
can do an exhaustive search two steps in.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:56.110
For example, let's say you opened with "soare",
and the pattern you happen to see was the

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:57.800
most likely one all greys's.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:03.310
Then you can run identical analysis from that
point for a given proposed second guess, something

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:07.930
like "kitty", what's the distribution across
all patterns in that restricted case where

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:11.400
we're restricted only to the words that would
produce all grays for "soare"?

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:17.031
And then we measure the flatness of that distribution
using this expected information formula, and

00:06:17.031 --> 00:06:22.340
we do that for all 13,000 possible words that
we could use as a second guess.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:26.710
Doing this, we can find the optimal second
guess in that scenario and the amount of information

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:28.730
we are expected to get from it.

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:32.629
And if we wash, rinse, and repeat and do this
for all of the different possible patterns

00:06:32.629 --> 00:06:37.560
that you might see, we get a full map of all
the best possible second guesses, together

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:44.090
with the expected information of each from
there.

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:48.330
If you take a weighted average of all those
second step values, weighted according to

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:52.919
how likely you are to fall into that bucket,
it gives you a measure of how much information

00:06:52.919 --> 00:06:57.500
you're likely to gain from the guest "soare"
after the second step.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:01.919
When we use this two-step metric as our new
means of ranking the list gets shaken up a

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:02.919
bit.

00:07:02.919 --> 00:07:06.520
"Soare" is no longer first place, it falls
back to 14th, and instead what rises to the

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.080
top is "slane".

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:15.060
Again, this doesn't feel very real, and it
looks like it is a British term for a spade

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.750
that's used for cutting turf.

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.229
All right.

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:22.639
But as you can see, it is a really tight race
among all of these top contenders for who

00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:25.120
gains the most information after those two
steps.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:29.949
And even still, these are not necessarily
the best opening guesses because information

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:33.620
is just the heuristic, it's not telling us
the actual score if you actually play the

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:34.650
game.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:41.289
What I did is I ran the simulation of playing
all 2315 possible Wordle games with all possible

00:07:41.289 --> 00:07:46.460
answers on the top 250 from this list.

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:51.110
And by doing this, seeing how they actually
perform, the one that ends up very marginally

00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:56.229
with the best possible score turns out to
be "salet".

00:07:56.229 --> 00:08:06.419
Which is... an alternate spelling for "Sallet",
which is a light medieval helmet.

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:11.629
All right, if that feels a little bit too
fake for you, which it does for me, you'll

00:08:11.629 --> 00:08:16.389
be happy to know that "Trace" and "Crate"
give almost identical performance, and each

00:08:16.389 --> 00:08:19.650
of them has the benefit of obviously being
a real word.

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:23.219
So there is one day when you get it right
on the first guess, since both are actual

00:08:23.219 --> 00:08:25.160
Wordle answers.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.240
This move from sorting based on the best two
step-entropy to sorting based on the lowest

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.720
average score also shakes up the list, but
not nearly as much.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:37.370
For example, "Salet" was previously third
place before it bubbles to the top, and "Crate"

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:39.660
and "Trace" were both fourth and fifth.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:44.120
If you're curious, you can get slightly better
performance from here by doing a little brute-forcing.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:47.710
There's a very nice blog post by Jonathan
Olsen if you're curious about this, where

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:51.710
he also lets you explore what the optimal
following guesses are for a few of the starting

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:55.350
words based on these optimal algorithms.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:09:00.230
Stepping back from all this, though, I'm told
by some people that it quote ruins the game

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:03.960
to overanalyze it like this and try to find
an optimal opening guess.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:08.760
It feels kind of dirty if you use that opening
guess after learning it, and it feels inefficient

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:09.760
if you don't.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.040
But the thing is, I don't actually think this
is the best opener for a human playing the

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.040
game.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:18.580
For one thing, you would need to know what
the optimal second guess is for each one of

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:20.320
the patterns that you see.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:24.970
And more importantly, all of this is in a
setting where we are absurdly overfit to the

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:26.700
official wordle answer list.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:31.600
The moment that, say, the New York Times chooses
to change what that list is under the hood,

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:33.630
all of this would go out the window.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:37.220
The way we humans play the game is just very
different from what any of these algorithms

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:38.220
are doing.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.650
We don't have the word list memorized, we
aren't doing exhaustive searches.

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:45.640
We get intuition from things like "what are
the vowels" and "how are they placed"?

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:49.670
I would actually be most happy if those of
you watching this video promptly forgot what

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:54.260
happens to be the technically best opening
guess and instead came out remembering things

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:58.550
like how do you quantify information, or the
fact that you should look out for when a greedy

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:02.780
algorithm falls short of the globally best
performance that you would get from a deeper

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:03.780
search.

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:07.710
To my taste, at least, the joy of writing
algorithms to try to play games actually has

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:11.360
very little bearing on how I like to play
those games as a human.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:15.600
The point of writing algorithms for all this
is not to affect the way you play the game,

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:16.850
it's still just a fun word game.

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:21.200
The point is to hone in our muscles for writing
algorithms in more meaningful contexts elsewhere.

